 "sensor": {
  "name": "FLI ProLine PL16803, unfiltered",
  "platform": "ground: AstriaNet node at New Mexico Skies (32.903 N, 105.530 W, 2225 m)",
  "band_nm": [
   350.0,
   1000.0
  ],
  "notes": "Gain 1.09 e-/ADU and read noise 10.3 e- by photon transfer over 157 pairs of frames up to 11 s apart (the frames are not bias-subtracted, so the read noise comes from the darkest, bias-only frames). Pixel scale, parity and distortion from frames carrying Gaia reference fits of their trail centres (mid-exposure), matched by trail centres. Trails 64 to 66 px long in the 3 s frames, from each frame's autocorrelation. PSF from the profile across 2935 isolated trails in 30 frames, which the tracking does not blur: a 2.02 px core with 50% at 3.6 px and 6% at 12.2 px (2.5 px FWHM across the trail). Zeropoint 21.448 at colour 0 through the atmosphere at airmass 1.16, the median of the 65 frames of 3 s (the night's first frames passed under cloud, which the median of frames leaves out), from boxes along the trails corrected for the halo outside them, and 0.2 x 1.16 more to above the atmosphere; colour term camera = G + 0.129 (BP-RP). The zeropoint drifts about 0.3 mag from G 7 to G 14 in these frames (fainter stars read fainter), so it holds to about 0.1 mag. Radiation from 40 frames of 3 s: each frame less its 5 x 5 median, star trails masked, events at pixels that fire in two or more frames left out: 15.7 per chip and frame, 61% in the upper half, which splits into 0.22 /cm2/s during the exposure and 4.4 s of read-out with the bottom row read first. Every event outside the masks counts, so the rate is an upper bound. Hot pixels from the 3 s frames only, 119 per Mpix above 2.9 e-/s at -20 C; the rates include the dark charge a row collects while it waits to be read. 9 um pixels given to the learner (--pixel-pitch-um); the headers lack them."
 },
